  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Tue Jun 30 2026\n\nThrough mid next week...\n\nOur area remains largely under the influence of a midlevel high over \nthe southeast CONUS this week with heat a primary concern in the \nshort term, and afternoon and evening convection each day. \nTemperatures in the mid 90s along with mid 70s dewpoints will \nsupport heat indices in the 105-110 range both today and tomorrow \nand a heat advisory continues areawide through late tomorrow.\n\nWith the center of the high situated over the area today, drier air \nwill limit convection for much of the area. There may be a little \ngreater moisture along with around 20kts of flow in the southeast \ntoday. This may be just enough to support isolated convection with \nan isolated wind gust, mainly this evening, and thus a \"marginal\" \nrisk has been highlighted. The caveat is the fact storms will be \nrunning into drier air as they push into our area, and it is \npossible that they stay just east of our area.\n\nThe combination of heat and humidity looks to be even greater \ntomorrow, particularly along and west of I-55 where convection is \noverall less likely, and/or convection arrives later. There is \nlikely a 5-6 hour window where heat warning criteria 110+ will be \nmet. An extreme heat warning and corresponding update of \n\"significant\" to this area in the graphic, have been issued.\n\nThe rest of the week, the midlevel high propagates northeastward, \nwhich introduces easterly flow and slightly lower heights. This \nweakness along with ample moisture should be enough to support \nwestward propagating convection. There will be a chance daily, \nhowever tomorrow, with slightly increased flow, there looks to be \nbetter chance for organization, though this looks marginal and \nloosely organized in most CAMs at the moment. An areawide marginal \nhas been added in the updated HWO graphics.\n\nBy the weekend, an upper low near the Canadian border will help to \nflatten and break down the ridge. With this, rain and storm chances \nshould increase with greater coverage this weekend into early next \nweek. All the while, heat stress could continue with temperatures in \nthe low 90s and dewpoints maintaining in the low 70s. Therefore, it \nis reasonable to assume depending on certain environmental \nparameters that severe could be possible and daily monitoring will \nbe needed. This hot and humid regime looks to persist through the \nrest of the period. /SAS/",
